The following are the predictions for House districts where at least one out of the [[Cook Political Report]], [[Daily Kos|Daily Kos Elections]], the [[Rothenberg Political Report]], [[Sabato's Crystal Ball]] and [[Real Clear Politics]] did not agree that the district was "safe Democratic" or "safe Republican." Incumbents not running for re-election have parentheses around their names, while incumbents with a caret (^) sought re-election, but were defeated in the primary election. Note that safeness of a district is not necessarily a prediction as to outcome.

195 seats are viewed as "safe Republican" and 159 as "safe Democratic" by all five of these sources — thus, out of the 81 seats listed below that at least one of these organizations views as competitive, Republicans would have to win 21 to reach a majority of 218 seats, and 37 in order to match their 2012 result of 234 seats; Democrats would have to win 59 in order to reach a majority of 218 seats, and 42 in order to match their 2012 result of 201 seats.

Voters will have the choice of only one major political party in more than one in six U.S. House elections nationwide, including more than one in four races in the Southern region.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://blog.lib.umn.edu/cspg/smartpolitics/2014/10/more_than_1_in_6_us_house_race.php |title= Blowout: More Than 1 in 6 US House Races Have Just One Major Party Nominee |work=Smart Politics |first=Eric |last=Ostermeier |date= October 7, 2014}}</ref>

